      "the refined visibility horizon (xid8 snapshot, SQL:visibility-horizon; D-visibility-horizon): the step-3 pull delivers the committed-and-visible seq 6 EVEN THOUGH the lower seq 5 is still in-flight (pg_visible_in_snapshot), and encodes the gap as the composite cursor '6~5': high-water 6, hole at the still-in-flight 5 (the opaque token, D-cursor-opaque-token; see ../../../spec/cursor-token.ts). has_more is true: the boundary stays open because the token carries a pending hole. the client stages seq 6 invisibly against the open boundary, so its durable checkpoint is still '4' at step 4 (no-intermediate-commit). after t1 resolves, the step-6 pull (re-issued from the durable '4' after the step-5 fault) delivers BOTH seq 5 and seq 6 and the cursor closes to the flat decimal '6': the late commit is delayed, never skipped",
      "the cursor encoding is what carries the gap: the high-water mark alone ('6') records no pending seq, so the composite token '6~5' is the delivery record this case pins (D-cursor-opaque-token, P:cursor-monotonicity-rebase-and-atomic-checkpoints)",
      "the transport fault at step 5 makes the client retry the poll from its committed checkpoint '4' (P:keyset-pagination-and-delivery-bound)",
      "the fenced global sequence may delay a late commit but never loses it (P:keyset-pagination-and-delivery-bound)"
